# PDF exports (DomPDF)

## Rule

Never render unbounded multi-document or large-table HTML as a single `Pdf::loadView` / `Pdf::loadHTML` call. DomPDF builds a full frame tree in memory and will OOM (seen at 512MB).

## Required pattern

1. **Single document** (one invoice, one receipt, one report card, one payslip) → DomPDF is fine.
2. **Bulk / list / multi-page school-wide** → use `App\Services\Pdf\ChunkedDompdfExporter` (or `PDFExportService::generateChunkedPDF` / `chunk_items` option).
3. Flow: **chunk → render → unset/gc → merge** (`qpdf` preferred on server; FPDI best-effort; ZIP last resort).
4. Apply `long_pdf_export` middleware on sync bulk routes.
5. Respect `config/pdf_export.php` caps (`chunk_size`, `sync_max_items`, `hard_max_items`). Do not “fix” OOMs by only raising `memory_limit`.

## Bad

```php
$pdf = Pdf::loadView('…bulk…', ['items' => $allHundreds])->stream();
```

## Good

```php
return app(ChunkedDompdfExporter::class)->stream($items, function ($chunk) use ($exporter) {
    return $exporter->renderView('…bulk…', ['items' => $chunk]);
}, ['filename' => 'export.pdf', 'chunk_size' => 10]);
```

## Prefer Excel

Huge tabular finance reports that users will filter in a spreadsheet should stay Excel-first; PDF is optional and must still be chunked.
